[size=5]Cartur's Portal Network[/size]

Choose where any portal goes. Open a portal, pick another portal from a list, and the first one opens onto it: no matching names, and the choice is kept when you save and reload.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jekkle/cartur/main/portal-network/images/portal-open.jpg[/img]
[i]A portal with a destination chosen. The opening shows a picture of the other portal's surroundings; cyan mist spills at the base.[/i]


[size=4]What it does[/size]

[list]
[*][b]Pick any destination.[/b] Interact with a portal to open the selection screen. It lists every portal in the world with its name, biome and distance. Search by name or biome, sort by distance or name, rename the portal you are at, activate a destination, or disconnect.
[*][b]A framed screen.[/b] The selection screen sits inside a carved steel frame with a tree, two ravens and a valknut worked into it. When it opens, light runs through the frame. While it is open the light slowly rises and falls. Rows light up under the pointer and buttons lift a little. Six rows show; a longer list scrolls. Pick a destination and the screen stays open for about a second while the light answers and the row turns ACTIVE. Closing dims the light from the bottom up. Arrow keys and Enter work in the list.
[*][b]Favourites, recent and Back.[/b] The diamond on a row marks a portal as a favourite; favourites stay at the top of the list. RECENT sorts by the portals you arrived at last. After a jump, BACK on the portal you came out of points it at the portal you came from. These are kept per character and world, in [b]BepInEx/config/com.cartur.portalnetwork.player.txt[/b].
[*][b]Show on map.[/b] MAP on a row closes the screen and opens the big map on that portal. (Cartur's Map Pins, if installed, already pins portals; this does not add pins.)
[*][b]A default destination.[/b] MAKE DEFAULT, next to the destination of the portal you are at, saves that destination; a Network portal you build afterwards starts pointed at it. Press again to clear it.
[*][b]The destination in the hover text.[/b] Looking at a portal with a chosen destination adds "Goes to <name>" under its name.
[*][b]Choices persist.[/b] A chosen destination survives saving, quitting and reloading, and survives renaming. If the destination is destroyed, the link is dropped.
[*][b]Portals that open.[/b] A portal with a destination stays dormant until a player comes within range (8 m by default), then its ring charges and the opening grows outward from the centre. Walking away closes it.
[*][b]A picture of the other side.[/b] The opening shows a snapshot of the destination, taken from in front of it while you stand near it. It is a picture, not a live view: nothing in it moves. The game keeps a day picture and a night picture of each destination and fades between them as the day turns (until a night picture exists, the day picture is darkened at night). Pictures refresh every ten minutes while you are close.
[*][b]A new portal piece.[/b] [i]Network portal[/i] is a carved portal with glowing runes. It is built on the normal portal, so it links, teleports and saves like one. It is added to the Hammer's list.
[*][b]Portal sounds.[/b] A portal makes an opening sound when it wakes, a steady hum while it is open, and a closing sound when it shuts. They replace the vanilla portal hum and are as loud as it by default. The portal someone just came out of stays quiet while it is closed.
[*][b]The jump is a short film.[/b] When you step into a Network portal the camera rushes at it: the view widens, tilts a little and shakes, speed streaks run out from the middle of the screen and the colours fringe (the game's own chromatic aberration), with a rising rush of sound. It stops in front of the portal, and the portal you walked into stands alone on black, with its swirl showing the other side, mist, drifting sparks and a slow push-in: that is the loading screen, and the portal's hum is the only sound while it lasts (the rest of the game's sound is paused and comes back on arrival). When the far side is ready, the camera rushes into the swirl, a flash and a boom, and you come out in front of the far portal with a burst of sparks. The camera starts at the very viewpoint the destination's picture was taken from, so what the window showed is what you come out into; after a second (your controls held) it cranes up over the portal and settles behind your character with a whoosh. In first person the arrival is shorter. The setting [b]PortalZoom[/b] turns it off. While menu animations are off, or while Windows has "Show animations in Windows" switched off (Windows does that by itself during a remote session such as Remote Desktop or TeamViewer), the jump is calm: no rush, streaks, shake, flashes, sparks or jump sounds, just a short fade to the portal on black with its hum, and a plain cut back.
[*][b]Seeing someone arrive.[/b] When the server approves a jump it tells the players near the exit portal (within about 120 m). The exit opens for them as a portal does when a player comes near, showing the picture of the place the traveller comes from, and the traveller appears in front of it a moment later (their camera flies at the portal for a second before the game's own teleport starts, which is what gives the exit time to open). Then it closes, because the traveller is the only one near it. It opens again, as any portal, as soon as another player comes near, and that player can use it. The traveller alone cannot walk back into it for 10 seconds, or until they have walked about 5 feet away, or until they open its menu (which is how they go back on purpose); a message tells them when it is refusing. Players without Portal Network see the ordinary portal.
[*][b]Faster short jumps.[/b] When the destination is already loaded, the jump takes about 2 seconds instead of vanilla's 8 or more. Long jumps are unchanged.
[*][b]Private portals.[/b] New portals are private to their builder on a server; share them with everyone, with chosen players or with a ward (see below).
[*][b]A server check.[/b] Before a jump, the server checks the portal is linked, the destination still exists, you are standing at the portal, and the world allows portals.
[*][b]Pictures come from the server.[/b] On a server, a picture of a destination taken by one player is kept by the server and sent to other players who may use that portal and have none of their own. A player who has never been near a destination still sees it in the opening.
[/list]

